This cheatsheet covers the accepted final Moth language design, including features beyond current compiler support.
See the @../progress (progress matrix) and @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/docs/roadmap/roadmap.md (roadmap) for implementation status.
Follow the linked references for ambiguous or detailed rules.

## Syntax and names

`:` opens a block and `;` closes it. Semicolons don't end statements. Indentation aids reading but doesn't define scope.
Statements run in source order.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    if ready:
        io.line("ready")
    else
        io.line("waiting")
    ;
]

- `--` starts a line comment in code. In template bodies it is text. Use `$note` or `$todo` for discarded template content.
- Names never shadow visible values, types, aliases, dependencies or builtins, including in parameters and child scopes.
  Separate scopes may reuse names when neither sees the other.
- Variables and functions use `snake_case`, types and generic parameters use `PascalCase` and traits use `ALL_CAPS`.
- Identifiers start with a letter or `_` and continue with letters, digits or `_`. Unicode letters are allowed.
  Leading underscores and changes of case can't escape reserved names, including `none`/`None`.
- Ordinary names such as `config`, `Config`, `project`, `block`, `group` and `region` are not keywords.
  `$config` and `@project` have their own meanings.
- There is no general bare lexical block or control-flow label. A `name:` statement opens a declared region. Exact `_:` is invalid.

Symbolic binary operators and assignment require spaces on both sides. Keep compound tokens together:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count ~= 0
    ~count = left + right
    ~count += 1
    ~count //= 2
    -- Invalid spacing: ~count=1, left+right, ~count+=1, count ~ = 0
]

## Values, bindings and constants

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    name = "Priya"  -- inferred and immutable
    age I32 = 30    -- explicitly typed
    ready Bool = true
    letter Char = 'm'

    count ~= 0            -- mutable
    names ~{String} = {}  -- mutable collection
    ~count = 1            -- reassignment, not a new binding

    site_name #= "Moth"  -- compile-time constant
    version #I32 = 1
]

`~` grants write access without changing the type. `#` requires a compile-time value and can't combine with `~`.
Ordinary declarations and constants need initialisers. Constants may use earlier same-file or imported constants,
without forward references or cycles.

### Strings and characters

Quoted text and templates share the `String` type. Use templates for interpolation and concatenation, rather than `String + String`:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    escaped = "Hello\nworld"
    greeting = [: Hello, [name].]
    joined = ["Hello, ", name]
    same = "Moth" is [: Moth]
]

Quoted strings support `\\`, `\"`, `\n`, `\r` and `\t`. They can't span physical lines. `is`/`is not` compare string contents.
Strings have no ordering, indexing or in-place character mutation. `Char` supports equality and ordering. `Bool` supports equality only.

### Const records

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    settings #= (
        title = "Moth",
        window = (width = 800, height = 600),
    )
    width #= settings.window.width
    empty_record #= ()
]

A const record is a compile-time group of named, folded values. Fields have unique labels with explicit values and don't see preceding field labels
as bindings. Runtime code may read its ordinary fields but can't pass, return or store the whole record or call methods on it.

Only Core functions with an explicit constant-evaluation contract can fold calls. MON shares record syntax but is a separate data format.

## References, copies and lifetimes

Existing values use shared read-only access when bound, passed, returned or stored. A live shared alias blocks overlapping mutation.
The compiler follows the *last use*, not just lexical scope.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    items ~= {1, 2}
    view = items    -- shared read-only alias
    size = view.length()
    ~items.push(3)  -- valid: view is no longer used

    independent ~= copy items  -- independent deep copy
    outer = {items}            -- new collection, shared inner storage
]

A mutable declaration from an existing mutable *place* creates a write-through alias.
A value returned from a function gets a separate caller binding, even if it shares storage:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    same_items |value {I32}| -> {I32}:
        return value
    ;

    original ~= {1}
    writer ~= original
    ~writer = {2}  -- also changes original

    returned ~= same_items(original)
    ~returned = {3}  -- original still holds {2}
]

`copy` deep-copies a binding, field or parenthesised place. It preserves internal sharing but shares no storage with the source.
Every member must be copyable. Opaque handles and resources need an independent-copy contract.
Literals, templates and computed results are not copyable places.

`~place` grants exclusive access for one operation, without copying or moving. Stored references must outlive their containers.
Field and element aliases also retain their containing storage. Moth has no explicit reference types, lifetime annotations or move operator.
Access and lifetime checks apply to every build profile.

Further detail: @../memory (Memory).

## Numbers and operators

- Signed integers: `I8`, `I16`, `I32`, `I64`
- Unsigned integers: `U8`, `U16`, `U32`, `U64`
- Binary floats: `F16`, `F32`, `F64`
- Decimal: `Dec`/`Dec0`, `Dec1` through `Dec256`
- `Byte`: an octet with comparison but no arithmetic

### Literals and inference

Whole literals default to `I32` and decimal/exponent literals to `F64` when unconstrained. Magnitude never widens a default.
An exact receiver can type a literal directly as `U64`, `F32`, `Dec` or another supported type.

**Moth infers unresolved numeric literals across an entire function body**, including later uses.
Annotations, parameters, return types, fields and writes can constrain them before defaults apply.
Unlike Rust's broader inference, later uses can't retype a completed Moth expression.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    accept_u64 |value U64|:
    ;

    count = 4_294_967_296
    accept_u64(count)  -- this later use resolves count as U64

    base I32 = 10
    total I64 = base + 1      -- base stays I32, raw 1 receives I64
    small U8 = 200
    samples {F32} = {1, 2.5}  -- raw mixed literals receive F32
    ordinary = 42             -- I32
    fraction = 0.5            -- F64

    seed = 1
    twice = seed + seed  -- completed I32 expression
    -- accept_u64(twice)      -- invalid: later receiver can't retype the addition
]

Raw numeric literals can inherit a type through arithmetic, collection elements and an established generic argument/result relationship.
Empty containers still need a type. Constants resolve when declared rather than from later runtime uses.

Use lowercase `e` and underscores between digits. Radix notation, suffixes, uppercase `E`, unary `+` and spaced unary minus are invalid.
Decimal/exponent literals can't initialise integers or `Byte`, even when whole. Unsigned types and `Byte` reject negative literals.

### Arithmetic and comparison

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    a I8 = 10
    b U8 = 20
    sum I16 = a + b       -- smallest signed type containing both ranges
    quotient F64 = a / a  -- / produces F64 for integers
    integral I8 = a // a  -- // truncates towards zero
]

Equal fixed integer types preserve their width (`I8 + I8 -> I8`). Mixed integer types use the smallest available type covering both operand ranges.
Thus `I8 + U8 -> I16`, while `I64 + U64` has no common arithmetic type. Fixed floats preserve the wider of their operand precisions.
Fixed integers and floats don't mix implicitly. `Dec` follows its separate scale rules below.

Arithmetic checks the **selected result type** at every step. Integer overflow is recoverable.
Binary floats round at their own precision and reject non-finite results, even for intermediate operations. Subnormals and signed zero are valid.
Fixed-integer comparisons remain exact, including `I64` against `U64`.

`//` truncates towards zero, `%` follows the dividend's sign and integer `/` returns `F64`.
Float division uses `/`. Unary `-` applies to signed numbers, not `U*` or `Byte`.

Cast operands **before** arithmetic when wider calculations are needed. Casting the result can't undo an earlier overflow.
Compound assignments check both the operation and destination write-back before committing. RHS `catch` doesn't handle that write-back.

### Byte and Dec

`Byte` accepts whole values from `0` to `255`. It supports equality and unsigned ordering, but not arithmetic or range iteration.
Convert through `U8` when doing numeric work.

`DecN` represents exact decimal scale `N`. `Dec` is `Dec0`. `Dec01` and scales greater than `256` are invalid.
Literals must fit their declared scale exactly.

- Same-scale addition, subtraction and negation are exact. Multiplication, positive-scale division and exponentiation round half to even at the
  result scale.
- Scale zero supports `//` and `%`, not `/`. Positive scales support `/` and `%`, not `//`.
- `DecN ^ I32` takes a non-negative exponent. Fixed integers combine exactly with the decimal operand's scale.
  Mixed decimal scales require an explicit cast.
- Binary floats and `Byte` never mix implicitly with `Dec`. Formatting uses ordinary decimal text without exponent notation.

See @../numbers (Numbers) for exact operator domains and inference rules.

## Explicit casts

`cast` converts between supported types, taking its target from an **immediate typed receiver** such as a declaration, return or parameter.
The operand keeps its own type. An untyped declaration or later use can't supply a missing cast target.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    octet U8 = 42
    count U64 = cast octet  -- infallible widening
    label String = cast count

    narrow_sum |left U32, right U32| -> U8:
        value U8 = cast (left + right) catch then 0
        return value
    ;
]

The `catch` above covers both addition overflow and narrowing failure. A cast of the *result* can't prevent earlier arithmetic from failing.

### Conversion rules

- Integer conversions preserve values and check range. Integer-to-float may round or fail when `F16` can't represent a finite result.
- Float-to-integer truncates towards zero, then checks range.
- Float precision narrowing rounds once to the target precision and fails if the result is not finite. Widening is infallible.
- Integers convert exactly to `DecN`, and widening decimal scale is exact. Narrowing scale or converting to integer requires exactness.
- Numeric text parsing must consume the complete string without surrounding whitespace. Formatting numbers as text is infallible.
- `String -> Bool` accepts `true` or `false` after trimming whitespace. `String -> Char` requires one Unicode scalar.
- `Byte` converts through `U8`. `Char` converts to/from its `I32` Unicode scalar value.
  `String -> Error` and `Error -> String` use the error message.

Fallibility depends on the **source and target types**, even for values known to fit.
Custom conversions use explicit trait conformance (see **Custom cast conformance**).

A `T?` cast target casts into `T` then wraps the result. Its handler must supply `T`, not `none`:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    parse_optional |text String| -> I32?:
        value I32 = cast text catch:
            return none
        ;
        return value
    ;
]

Fallible casts follow ordinary implicit failure rules: handle them with `catch` or builtin `Error!`.
Custom error types need explicit recovery or mapping. Postfix `!` is for declared fallible calls.

## Functions and calls

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    describe |prefix String = "item", subject String| -> String:
        return [: [prefix]: [subject]]
    ;

    label = describe(subject = "apple")
    other_label = describe("fruit", subject = "pear")
]

Parameters use `|...|`, or `||` when empty. Call with parentheses. Positional arguments precede named ones.
A named argument may skip a default, which must be compile-time evaluable and can't refer to other parameters.
Builtin and host calls use positional arguments. Omit `->` for no success or error results. A function with only a failure slot writes `-> Error!`.

Mutable parameters require `~place` for existing mutable storage, including named arguments. Fresh values and explicit copies stay unmarked:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    increment |value ~I32|:
        ~value += 1
    ;

    count ~= 1
    increment(~count)
    increment(value = ~count)
    increment(copy count)
    increment(1)
]

Shared parameters reject `~`. Mutable receiver calls require `~` on an existing mutable place and can't use a fresh value as the receiver.

Multiple return slots remain **separate results, not a tuple**:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    pair || -> String, I32:
        return "Priya", 2
    ;

    name, count = pair()
]

Receive every result through a matching multi-bind, return or value-producing block.
Prefix each existing target with `~`, as in `~count, message = next()`. Unmarked targets define new bindings, while prefixed targets update existing mutable bindings.
All right-hand results evaluate once before writes to existing targets. Every reachable success path must return the declared values or terminate.
There is no `Void` type and a no-success function may fall through.

### Never-returning calls: `-> !`

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    fatal |message String| -> !:
        assert(false, message)
    ;

    require_name |ready Bool| -> String:
        name = if ready:
            then "Priya"
        else
            fatal("name unavailable")
        ;
        return name
    ;
]

`-> !` declares a function that never returns normally. It replaces the return contract, not a value type.
Every reachable path must diverge. Propagating an error or option doesn't count.
These calls terminate their statement or branch and can't be received as values or handled with `!`, `?` or `catch`.

## Options: `T?`, `none` and postfix `?`

`T?` holds either `T` or `none`, without an `Option` constructor. `none` requires an immediate optional type context.
Optional values never unwrap implicitly.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    find_name |id String| -> String?:
        if id is "":
            return none
        ;
        return "Priya"
    ;

    load_label |id String| -> String?:
        name = find_name(id)?  -- absent: return none immediately
        return [: User: [name]]
    ;
]

Postfix `?` unwraps or propagates absence. Its function needs exactly one compatible optional success slot. It can't combine with `catch`.
Inspect or capture a present value with `is`:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    maybe_name String? = "Priya"
    label = if maybe_name is |name| then name else "guest"

    if maybe_name is none:
        io.line("missing")
    ;

    if maybe_name is:
        "Priya" => io.line("admin")
        |name| => io.line(name)
        none => io.line("guest")
    ;
]

Options support equality when their inner type does, but no ordering. There is no general fallback operator. Use a producing `if` or match.

## Errors, propagation and recovery

A final `E!` result slot declares typed failure. `return!` returns an error, postfix `!` propagates a compatible error and `catch` handles it.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    load_user |id String| -> String, F64, Error!:
        if id is "":
            return! Error("Missing id", 404)
        ;
        return "Priya", 1.0
    ;

    load_summary |id String| -> String, Error!:
        name, score = load_user(id)!
        return [: [name]: [score]]
    ;

    name, score = load_user("") catch |err|:
        io.warn(err.message)
        then "guest", 0.0
    ;
]

`Error` has `message String` and `code U32 = 0`. Code `0` is still an error. Custom error types are ordinary nominal types.
An error-only `-> Error!` function succeeds by reaching the end. Error channels aren't first-class `Result` values.
Use a choice for domain-level alternatives.

### Implicit failure

Checked numeric operations and casts can fail even without a written `E!` on the immediate expression.
Within a module, **private functions can propagate these failures without postfix `!` at every private call**.
A builtin `Error!` boundary turns them into `Error`. An exported function without an error slot must handle them,
while a custom `E!` requires local recovery or explicit mapping.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    product |left I32, right I32| -> I32:
        return left * right  -- implicit failure can travel through private calls
    ;

    export:
        checked_product |left I32, right I32| -> I32, Error!:
            return product(left, right)
        ;

        product_or_zero |left I32, right I32| -> I32:
            return product(left, right) catch then 0
        ;
    ;
]

These boundaries also apply to methods, generic functions and re-exports. Ordinary top-level entry work has a builtin `Error!` contract.
A transparent alias of `Error` retains builtin behaviour, but a custom wrapper doesn't.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    GeometryError ::
        InvalidDimensions,
    ;

    area |width I32, height I32| -> I32, GeometryError!:
        result = width * height catch:
            return! GeometryError::InvalidDimensions
        ;
        return result
    ;
]

### Whole-expression catch

`catch` protects the selected **whole expression**, including nested calls, receivers and arithmetic, rather than only the last operation.
It evaluates work once, stops at the first failure and preserves earlier side effects. A handler's own failure propagates outward.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    increment_or_zero |value I8| -> I8:
        return value + 1 catch then 0
    ;
]

`catch` is valid for casts, arithmetic, actual recoverable failures and expressions containing at least two calls at the selected level.
For example, `source.first().second()` qualifies, while `outer(inner())` needs an actually fallible call.
The compiler may still prove an eligible expression infallible.

Use `catch then value`, `catch |err| then value`, `catch:` or `catch |err|:`.
Handlers supply matching values, return or diverge. Their error binding is immutable and local.
`catch` handles compatible errors, not assertions, allocation exhaustion or unexpected host faults.

For compound assignment, RHS `catch` doesn't cover the update itself.
To recover the entire update, compute the replacement and any narrowing cast under `catch` before assigning, or use a helper.

### Assertions

`assert` checks invariants in every build and aborts unrecoverably on failure:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    assert(ready)
    assert(condition = ready, message = "must be ready")
]

`assert` is statement-only, with a `Bool` condition and optional `String?` message. Omission uses `"assertion failed"`.
The message is checked even when the condition is statically true, but evaluated only on failure.
Handle failing message expressions first. Assertion messages can't propagate errors or return.
`assert(false)` terminates, and assertion failures can't be caught.

## Branching and value-producing blocks

Conditions use `Bool`. A statement `else` is optional. Nest an `if` or use matching instead of `else if`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    label = if ready then "ready" else "waiting"

    score_label = if score is:
        < 0 => then "invalid"
        0 => then "zero"
        >= 90 => then "excellent"
        else => then "other"
    ;

    label, score = if ready:
        then "ready", 1
    else
        io.warn("waiting")
        then "waiting", 0
    ;
]

`then` supplies results to an immediate declaration, assignment or return, including multiple slots.
Value-producing blocks can't appear inside calls, collections or other expressions. Every reachable path must produce matching results or terminate.
A producing `if` needs an `else`. Inline `then ... else ...` stays on one logical line.

A full match uses `if value is:` with `pattern =>` arms, optionally guarded by `if condition`. `else =>` is the catch-all.
A bodyless `else =>` ignores remaining cases in a statement match.

- Ordinary matches need `else =>`. A choice match may omit it when all variants appear unguarded. A guarded arm requires `else =>`.
- An option match may omit it with both unguarded `none` and `|name|` arms. Literal matches alone don't cover every present value.
- Relational patterns work with numbers, `Byte` and `Char`, not `String`. Patterns match the subject's type.

A compile-time-known `if` selects its branch only after both sides have been checked. This doesn't remove declarations, exports or file dependencies.

## Loops

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count ~= 0
    names = {"Priya", "Rob"}

    loop count < 3:  -- condition checked before each iteration
        ~count += 1
    ;

    loop names |name, index|:  -- read-only element and optional I32 index
        io.line([: [index]: [name]])
    ;

    loop 0 to 10 by 2 |value|:
        io.line([: [value]])
    ;
]

Collection loops capture their source and length once. The iteration binding is immutable, and a map is not a collection-loop source.
`break` exits the nearest loop, `continue` advances it and neither takes values or labels.

Ranges exclude the endpoint with `to` and include it with `to &`. `loop to n` begins at zero.
`by` supplies the step magnitude. Direction follows the bounds. Integer steps default to one, while float ranges require `by`.
Bounds and steps evaluate once. Zero steps fail, as do floating steps that can't make progress.
Updates respect fixed-width limits, including inclusive endpoints. `Byte` and `Dec` have no range iteration.

### Repeated option capture

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    loop ~scanner.next_token() is |token|:
        inspect(token)
    ;
]

An option-producing header runs before every attempted iteration, binding one immutable present payload or exiting on `none`.
`continue` reevaluates the header, while `break` doesn't. It implies no special `next` method or consumption rule.

### Collection-producing loops

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    positives {I32} = loop {-2, 1, 3} |item|:
        if item > 0:
            then item
        ;
    ;
]

Any ordinary loop can eagerly produce `{T}` for a declaration, assignment or single-value return.
`then value` appends once and ends that iteration. `continue` and fallthrough append nothing. `break` returns the partial collection.
At least one reachable `then` is required. An explicit `{T}` supplies element type context.

A nested ordinary loop blocks the outer production target. A nested producing loop has its own. Templates don't have collection-producing loops.

## Structs and receiver methods

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Person = | name String, age I32 = 0 |

    birthday |this ~Person|:
        ~this.age += 1
    ;

    display_name |this Person| -> String:
        return this.name
    ;

    person ~= Person(name = "Priya", age = 30)
    ~person.age += 1
    ~person.birthday()  -- mutating receiver needs ~
    person_name = person.display_name()
]

Fields have explicit types. Defaults must fold and omitted non-default fields are errors. Constructors use normal arguments.
Identical fields don't make distinct structs compatible. Field mutation requires mutable access.

Receiver methods are top-level functions starting with `this T` or `this ~T`, defined in the receiver type's file.
Methods are visible wherever their receiver type is visible. They can't be imported or exported on their own.
Mutable methods need `~place.method(...)`.

### Runtime anonymous records

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    show_state |title String|:
        child = (count = 3)
        state ~= (
            title = title,
            saved = child,
            fresh = (enabled = true),
        )
        ~state.fresh.enabled = false
        independent = copy state
        count = independent.saved.count
    ;
]

Runtime records use non-empty named fields, evaluated once in source order.
Field labels aren't local bindings, type annotations or receiving contexts.
Context-sensitive values such as `none`, `cast` and empty collections need an earlier typed value. Empty `()` is a const record only.

**Each runtime record literal site has its own type**, even when fields match. Repeated execution, aliases and copies retain that identity.
Records can nest and be copied locally. Hidden record types can't cross function arguments/results, nominal fields, exports,
collection/map storage or generic arguments.
Extracted ordinary fields retain their usual types.

## Choices

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Status ::
        Ready,
        Loading | progress F64 |,
        Failed | message String, code I32 |,
    ;

    ready = Status::Ready
    failed Status = ::Failed(message = "offline", code = 503)

    if failed is:
        Ready => io.line("ready")
        Loading(progress) => io.line([: [progress]%])
        Failed(message as reason, code) => io.error([: [code]: [reason]])
    ;
]

A choice needs at least one variant. Unit variants omit `()`. Payloads need non-empty, explicitly typed immutable fields, without defaults.
Construction supplies every field through normal argument routing. Known expected choice types permit `::Variant(...)`, otherwise qualify it.
Write `Choice::Variant` with no whitespace around `::`.

Patterns capture every exact field name in declaration order. `as` renames the immutable arm-local binding.
Wildcards and named assignments are invalid. See **Branching** for guards/exhaustiveness.

Same-type equality compares tag and payload only when every possible payload type supports equality.
Scalars, decimals, qualifying choices/options work. Structs, collections, maps, functions and opaque types don't.
One unsupported field disables whole-choice equality, including unit variants. Choices have no ordering.

## Type aliases and generics

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    UserId as I32
    Names as {String}
    MaybeName as String?
    User = | id I32 |
]

Aliases are transparent (`UserId` is `I32`). Named structs and choices have their own identity regardless of matching fields.
Aliases may chain but not cycle and they create no new constructors.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    identity type A |value A| -> A:
        return value
    ;

    Box type A = | value A |
    Maybe type A ::
        Some | value A |,
        Empty,
    ;

    get type A |this Box of A| -> A:
        return this.value
    ;

    empty type A || -> {A}:
        return {}
    ;

    accept_u64 |value U64|:
    ;

    box Box of String = Box("Moth")
    value = identity(1)
    accept_u64(value)      -- later use resolves an existing numeric origin
    items {I32} = empty()  -- immediate receiver supplies the generic result
    missing Maybe of String = Maybe::Empty
]

Generics apply to top-level structs, choices and free functions.
Function type arguments come from call arguments or an immediately expected result, such as `items {I32} = empty()`.
As with ordinary numeric inference, an unresolved numeric literal can gain its type from later uses when the signature preserves it.
Generic inference uses the declared signature, rather than the callee's body.

Use `of` to name concrete generic types. Nested `of` applications need an alias:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Pair type A, B = | first A, second B |
    StringIntPair as Pair of String, I32
    paired Box of StringIntPair = Box(Pair("count", 3))
]

`{Box of String}` is valid with a single `of` application. Generic receiver methods use their type's generic parameters.
Generic values can be passed, stored and returned without bounds:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    make_box type A |value A| -> Box of A:
        return Box(value)
    ;

    boxed Box of String = make_box("hello")
]

Operations that depend on `A`, such as comparing or displaying it, need trait bounds (see **Traits and conformance**).

## Traits and conformance

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    DISPLAY_TEXT must:
        display |This| -> String
    ;

    Label = | text String |

    display |this Label| -> String:
        return this.text
    ;

    Label must DISPLAY_TEXT

    render type Item is DISPLAY_TEXT |item Item| -> String:
        return item.display()
    ;
]

Traits are **compile-time contracts**. A type conforms with `Type must TRAIT` beside its declaration and matching methods.
Requirements declare signatures without bodies. Generic bounds use `is`, as in `type Item is DISPLAY_TEXT and NAMED`.
Conformance travels with the type, so callers can use bounded functions without importing the trait separately.

Requirements use `This` or `~This` receivers and may return `This` directly, but can't wrap `This` in another type.
Signatures must match, including access and results.
Traits are resolved statically. Use choices to represent runtime alternatives between concrete types.

`READABLE must not WRITABLE` makes the traits incompatible.

### Custom cast conformance

A method alone doesn't enable a user-defined cast. The source type must explicitly conform to a built-in cast trait:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    to_string |this Label| -> String:
        return this.text
    ;

    Label must CASTABLE_TO_STRING

    label = Label("Moth")
    text String = cast label
]

`CASTABLE_TO_I32` requires `to_i32 |This| -> I32`. `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_I32` requires `try_to_i32 |This| -> I32, Error!`.
Other fixed numeric targets follow the same pattern, alongside supported `Bool`, `String`, `Char` and `Error` casts.
A source/target pair can conform to only one of the infallible or fallible variants. `Byte` and `Dec` have no custom numeric casts.

## Collections

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    names ~= {"Priya", "Rob"}       -- growable {String}
    empty ~{I32} = {}               -- typed empty collection
    fixed ~{3 I32} = {10, 20}       -- capacity 3
    capacity #I32 = 4
    labels {capacity} = {"a", "b"}  -- fixed capacity, inferred element type

    size = names.length()
    ~names.push("Emmy")
    first = names.get(0)  -- shared read-only element
    ~names.set(0, "Huw")
    removed = ~names.remove(1)
    ~names.clear()
]

`{T}` grows dynamically. `{N T}` has fixed maximum capacity `N`, which is part of its type. `N` is a positive `I32` literal or bare `#I32` constant.
Length, capacity and indexes use `I32`. Collections have no `[]` indexing or builtin equality.

Non-empty literals have one element type. Raw numeric literals can receive that type from context.
A collection can't promote already typed elements to a new storage type. Empty collections require an explicit type at declaration or field default.

`get` returns a shared element. `set` replaces, `push` appends, `remove` returns and shifts elements and `clear` empties the collection.
Invalid indexes fail without changing contents. Fixed `push` fails when full. Growable `push` only fails fatally on allocation exhaustion.
Use normal `Error!` handling for recoverable collection failures. Live element aliases block conflicting mutation.

## Hash maps

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    scores ~{String = I32} = {"Priya" = 10, "Rob" = 8}
    empty_scores ~{String = I32} = {=}

    found = scores.contains("Priya")
    score = scores.get("Priya") catch then 0
    ~scores.set("Emmy", 5) catch:
        io.warn("update failed")
    ;
]

`{Key = Value}` uses one key/value form throughout. `{}` is a collection and `{=}` is an empty map.
Keys may be `String`, `Bool`, `Char`, `Byte` or a fixed-width integer, but not floats, `Dec` or user-defined types.
Types are exact. Numeric promotion can't change an existing key or value type.

`get` fails for a missing key, `contains` returns `Bool` and `set` inserts or replaces.
`remove` returns and removes a value, while `clear` empties the map. `length` is an `I32` property **without parentheses**.
Keys are borrowed and `get` returns a shared read-only value, so a live alias blocks conflicting mutation.

Replacing a key preserves its position. Removing and reinserting moves it to the end.
Maps have no square-bracket indexing or collection-loop iteration. Nesting beyond two inline map levels needs an alias.

## Declared regions

Most lifetimes are inferred. Declare a region when allocations need an explicit shared lifetime, particularly for reference cycles or bulk cleanup.
Only `into name` places newly created storage in a region. Being lexically inside one is not enough.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    request:
        scratch:
            temporary String into scratch = [: temporary]
            saved String into request = copy temporary
        ;

        io.line(saved)
    ;
]

`into` targets the current or an enclosing region and places fresh storage or an independent `copy`.
Placing into an ancestor requires straight-line nesting. For conditional construction, use a value-producing `if`,
match or `catch` at the destination.

Storage is reclaimed together when its region exits, including on error or early return.
Region-owned values can't escape or retain shorter-lived storage. A reference cycle must belong entirely to one declared region.
Reassignment preserves region ownership. Use `copy` to introduce independent storage from another region.

Regions are neither values nor types. See @../memory (Memory) for full placement and lifetime rules.

## Templates

Templates produce `String` values. Static content folds at compile time. Dynamic interpolations evaluate at runtime.
A `:` separates the head from the body.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    content = [$md:
        # Hello [name]
    ]
    joined = ["Hello, ", name]  -- concatenation, no String + String
    literal = [: ["[brackets]"]]
]

A `.mtf` source begins as an implicit compile-time `$md` body exported as `content #String`. See @../moth-templates (Moth template files).

### Directives and formatting

Directives are compiler or builder-provided operations written `$name` or `$name(arguments)`.
Some format templates, while others configure projects or mark declarations (see @../directives (Directives)).

`$md` formats Markdown. `$raw` preserves text and whitespace. `$note`/`$todo` discard content, while `$doc` marks documentation.
The HTML builder supplies `$html`, `$css` and `$escape_html`. `$code("language")` highlights literal code.
Formatters apply to their own templates, and incompatible formatters can't share a head.

### Slots and wrappers

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    card #= [:
        <h1>[$slot("title")]</h1>
        <section>[$slot]</section>
    ]
    [card:
        [$insert("title"): Welcome]
        Hello, [name].
    ]

    image #= [: <img src="[$slot(1)]" alt="[$slot]">]
    [image, "logo.png": Moth logo]

    list #= [$children([:<li>[$slot]</li>]): <ul>[$slot]</ul>]
    [list: [: one] [$fresh: [: two]]]
]

Named `$slot` values receive matching `$insert` content. Numbered slots consume head contributions in order. `$slot` receives the remainder.
Missing slots render empty, repeated slots reuse content and unassigned content is an error.
`$children` wraps direct children. `$fresh` skips one parent wrapper.

### Template control flow

Templates support a single-body `if` and `loop`, written last in the head. More complex decisions belong in ordinary Moth code.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    [card, if maybe_name is |name|:
        Hello [name]
    ]

    [loop items |item, index|:
        [index]: [item]
    ]
]

An `if` body renders when its condition is true or its option is present, with a captured value available only in that body.
Loops concatenate iteration bodies in order, using conditional, collection or range headers.
Template control flow supports `if` and `loop` only, without `else`, `break` or `continue`.

A loop-head wrapper wraps the whole loop once. Place per-item wrappers inside the body.
Const templates check every body for compile-time evaluation, including inactive branches.
Constant loops have a configurable iteration limit.

### Moth Markdown

`$md` supports headings, paragraphs, lists, emphasis, links and same-line paired backtick code, while escaping raw HTML.
Use `$code` for code blocks and `@html` helpers for tables. For HTML projects, import `@html table, th, td` in `.moth` files.
The same helpers are available automatically in `.mtf` files:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    [table, [th: [: Name] [: Score]]:
        [td: [: Priya] [: 10]]
    ]
]

Static headings receive kebab-case IDs, linkable with `@#hello (Hello)`.

Links use `@./path (label)`, `@/route (label)` or `@https://example.com (label)`.
The target and label may be separated by spaces, tabs and **at most one newline** (including CRLF). Other content breaks the link.
Link targets don't themselves create file dependencies.

See @../templates (Templates).

## Wiring

`Wire` passes the identity of a named binding. `Route` captures a choice constructor for a later input or event.
Both are parameter contracts, so a UI or other consumer can decide how to use them.

### Wires

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    read_name |value Wire of String| -> String:
        return value
    ;

    forward_name |value Wire of String| -> String:
        return read_name(value)
    ;

    name ~= "Priya"
    label = forward_name(name)
]

`Wire of T` accepts a named immutable/mutable local or compatible wire parameter and preserves its identity through reassignment.
Reading it as `T` uses ordinary **shared read-only** access. Only whole named bindings can form wires.

### Routes

A route allows a consumer to turn input into an ordinary choice message. This example shows the parameter interface a UI builder might expose.
The `text_input` and `button` functions below stand in for UI components. They show route formation, not a built-in event runtime.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    TodoMessage ::
        Rename | id I32, text String |,
        Delete | id I32 |,
    ;

    text_input |value Wire of String, on_change Route of String -> TodoMessage|:
        io.line(value)  -- illustrative UI component
    ;

    button |label String, on_click Route of TodoMessage|:
        io.line(label)  -- illustrative UI component
    ;

    update_title |current String, message TodoMessage| -> String:
        return if message is:
            Rename(id, text) => then text
            Delete(id) => then ""
        ;
    ;

    todo_id = 42
    title ~= "Draft"
    text_input(title, TodoMessage::Rename(todo_id))
    button("Delete", TodoMessage::Delete(todo_id))

    -- When the text input supplies "Revised", the route produces this message:
    changed = TodoMessage::Rename(42, "Revised")
    ~title = update_title(title, changed)
]

`Route of String -> TodoMessage` captures `todo_id` once. The future `String` fills the remaining `text` field.
The resulting message can be handled by ordinary Moth code, as `update_title` demonstrates.
`Route of TodoMessage` captures a complete constructor, so the Delete action needs no future input.
Compatible route parameters can forward routes unchanged.

Wires and routes can only appear as parameter capabilities. Their reads and captures follow ordinary access and lifetime rules.
UI event delivery needs a builder or library that implements it.

See @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/docs/src/docs/wiring/wiring.mtf (Wiring contract).

## Dependencies and aliases

Moth has no `import` keyword. Use a top-level `@path` to bind names or namespaces:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @core/math sin, cos, PI
    @components render as render_component, Button as UiButton
    @core/text as text
    @vendor/drawing.js as drawing
]

`as` after one selection renames that binding. After the path it binds a namespace without selections.
Bare `@core/math` binds `math`, used as `math.sin(1.0)`. Namespaces are not first-class values. Selections are flat names, not path components.

Paths resolve relative to the **module root**, not the importing file. Omit `.moth`, `.mtf` and `.md` extensions for source dependencies,
but retain extensions for providers such as `.js`. A path may bind an owned file, visible child module or support package.
Module boundaries expose only exports. `@./`, `@/` and `../` are invalid dependency paths.
Root filenames (`@*.moth`, `+*.moth`) can't be imported directly.

Selections are file-local and can't shadow existing names. Commas permit multiline selection lists, with the path and first selection on the same
line. Source clauses bind available providers. They don't acquire or install packages.

See @../packages (Packages and dependencies).

## File values and resources

An explicit-extension file value is an ordinary `String`, not a separate path type:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    logo #= @assets/logo.svg
    image #= [$html:<img src="[logo]" alt="Moth">]
]

`.mtf` and `.md` provide content strings. Other accepted extensions provide resource strings that can be stored, passed, returned and exported.
`.moth` has **no file value**. Paths resolve from the module root and can't cross private module or package boundaries.
Export a String to share a resource across them.

Resource URLs receive their final characters from the consuming output. Constants can carry resources, but compile-time operations can't inspect an
unresolved URL. Physical targets must exist even in inactive branches. Only resources actually used in output are emitted.

Bare `@/` is a site-root string, not a file or valid dependency path. Ordinary absolute paths, `@./`, parent traversal and physical file values in
`config.moth` are invalid.

See @../resources (File paths and resource values).

## Modules, exports and packages

A directory-scoped `@*.moth` is a normal module root. The filename suffix is cosmetic.
Ordinary `.moth` files and unrooted subdirectories belong to that module. Other modules see only what its root exports:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    private_label #= "Moth"

    export:
        greeting #= "Hello"

        render || -> String:
            return [: [greeting], [private_label]]
        ;

        @core/math PI
    ;
]

A root can have one non-empty `export:` block for public declarations and direct re-exports.
Runtime statements, fragments and whole namespaces aren't exports. Public signatures can't expose private types or required private trait evidence.
Methods are visible wherever their receiver type is visible. They can't be imported or exported separately.

A `+*.moth` root creates a named support package, such as `src/shared/+package.moth` exposed as `@shared`.
Its `export:` facade lets normal sibling modules share code, while preserving private implementation boundaries.
It is visible to its nearest normal owner and normal siblings/descendants in that scope.

An optional project-root `+*.moth` beside `config.moth` exposes the project's **external** package API. Internal modules can't import this facade.
Neither exported signatures nor reachable exported implementations may depend on private `@project` values.

For example, a project might contain:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    my-site/
      config.moth
      src/
        @site.moth
        helpers.moth
        tools/format.moth
        vendor/format.js
        shared/
          +package.moth
          badge.moth
        pages/
          @pages.moth
          article/
            @article.moth
]

From `src/@site.moth` (or `src/helpers.moth`), imports can bind:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @tools/format format_title   -- ordinary file, same module
    @pages page_heading          -- child module's exported name
    @shared badge                -- support package's exported name
    @core/math PI                -- Core package
    @html table                  -- HTML builder package
    @charts plot                 -- registered external dependency
    @vendor/format.js emphasize  -- builder-provided JS binding
]

The nested `src/pages/article/@article.moth` can import the same support package and Builder packages:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @shared badge
    @html table
]

Even an ordinary file nested under `src/tools/` imports `@tools/format`, because paths always start at its module root.
The `@pages` and `@shared` imports see only their exported facades.
`@charts` assumes a declared project dependency. The `.js` binding requires an HTML builder capable of importing JavaScript.

See @../project-structure (Project structure) and @../packages (Package visibility).

## Directives and project configuration

`$name(arguments)` invokes a registered compile-time directive. With no arguments, write the bare directive (`$html_builder`), not empty parentheses.
Directives are provided by the compiler or selected builder, not defined in source.

Project-root `config.moth` contains compile-time values and configuration. It requires one `$project` and the selected builder's config.
Source imports, functions and runtime work belong in ordinary modules:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $config
    version #String = "0.1.0"

    $project(name = "my_site", version = version)
    $html_builder
]

`$project` requires `name` and `version` Strings. Its `entry_root` defaults to `"src"` beneath the project root. Only predefined fields are accepted.
`$html_builder` configures HTML output, not source-level target selection. Its default output folders are `dev` and `release`, with `origin = "/"`.

### Build inputs

`$config` marks the next **explicitly typed top-level constant** as a build input. The initializer supplies a fallback.
Without one, an ordinary input is required while an optional input defaults to `none`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $config
    api_url #String

    $config
    analytics #Bool = false

    $config
    label #String?
]

Inputs support `String`, `Bool`, `Char`, exact fixed-width integers, binary floats and one optional layer.
`Byte`, `Dec`, mutable bindings and inferred `#=` declarations can't be inputs.
Source-module input defaults must be primitive literals or `none`, while `config.moth` may use earlier compile-time helpers.

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    moth build . --input api_url=https://example.com --input analytics=true
    moth build . --input count=4_294_967_296
    moth build . --input 'label="true"'
]

CLI numeric spellings are resolved directly against their declared exact type, without an `I32`/`F64` intermediary.
Quotes distinguish a String such as `"true"` from the Bool `true`. Inputs sharing a name in a project/package must agree on type,
optionality and fallback. Dependencies resolve their own inputs.

Project metadata is available only through explicit imports:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @project version
    footer #= [: Version [version]]
]

`@project` exposes predefined fields, not arbitrary config helpers or inputs.
Input-dependent values can't determine `name`, `entry_root` or `template_const_loop_iteration_limit`.

See @../project-structure/#project-config (Project configuration), @../project-structure/#build-inputs (Build inputs) and
@../directives (Directives).

## Commands and project builders

[codeblock, $code("bash"):
    moth new html my-site
    cd my-site
    moth check .
    moth dev .
    moth build . --release
]

`check` validates the project and required target capabilities without writing output. `dev` serves the development build.
`build --release` writes configured release output.

Modular project builders supply packages, capabilities and output conventions. HTML turns `$page` roots into documents and routes.
Other builders, such as Lappet, supply their own packages and decide how to consume top-level work.
Source can't query the OS, architecture, backend or physical target. The selected target must support every reachable required capability.

## Pages and top-level work

The **HTML project builder** uses `$page` to mark a normal module root as a page and activate its top-level runtime work or direct output.
Other builders can define their own entry or runtime purpose directives instead.

For an HTML page:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    $page(title = "Moth")

    #[$md:
        # Welcome
    ]

    name = "Moth"
    [: Hello, [name].]
]

`$page` applies to the whole root. It accepts compile-time optional `title`, `description`, `lang`, `favicon`, `body_style` and `head` settings.
Helpers, support roots, facades and `config.moth` can't declare pages. Declaration-only roots and documentation/comment templates need no page
purpose.

Direct constant fragments become static HTML. Runtime work runs once at page startup, and runtime fragments publish only after successful completion.
An error leaves static fragments in place but doesn't roll back earlier side effects.
The release page shows a generic failure notice, not application error text.

Routes derive from directory structure: `src/guide/@page.moth` creates `guide/index.html`. Root filenames have no route significance.
HTML builds need at least one page. `check` can validate a declaration-only, zero-page project while still rejecting unpurposed runtime work.

See @../project-structure (Pages and entry behaviour).

## Core IO and foreign functions

The prelude exposes `io` from `@core/io`: `io.print`, `io.line`, `io.debug`, `io.warn` and `io.error` accept a single `String`.
Format other types with templates:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count = 3
    io.line([: Count: [count]])
]

`io.set_title(text)` changes the host's displayed title when the selected environment supports it.
For HTML it updates the live document title without rewriting static page metadata.

Foreign packages depend on the selected builder and target. For example, the HTML builder can bind JavaScript files to typed Moth functions:

[codeblock, $code("js"):
    /**
     * @moth.sig emphasize |text String| -> String
     */
    export function emphasize(text) {
        return `**${text}**`;
    }
]

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @vendor/format.js emphasize

    label |text String| -> String:
        return emphasize(text)
    ;
]

Annotations declare typed Moth functions independently of JavaScript export names.
They accept positional typed parameters, up to one success result and an optional `Error!`.
Foreign calls check supported value conversions, including exact numeric types and finite float results.
`@moth.opaque` declares foreign handles and `@moth.const` declares supported static exports.
Foreign code can't retain borrowed Moth storage.

JavaScript bindings provide typed free functions without callbacks or async. Use ordinary Moth wrapper types for methods.
Other builders need their own providers and supported ABIs. Lappet, for example, doesn't execute JavaScript.

Annotated `.wat` files use the same `@moth.sig` signatures for WebAssembly text functions, where the builder provides a suitable ABI adapter.
WIT describes a separate foreign Wasm component boundary. Native Moth packages use Moth interfaces rather than WIT.

See @../packages/core/io (Core IO) and @../packages (Foreign binding contracts).

## Further reference

For full syntax and semantic details, see @../language-overview (Language overview), @../memory (Memory) and the topic references above.
For deliberately excluded features and future language directions, see @../design-scope (Design scope).